1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,013
A cinema ticket says row F, seat twelve.

2
00:00:03,013 --> 00:00:05,476
A letter says street, then door number.

3
00:00:05,476 --> 00:00:07,758
A train ticket gives coach and berth.

4
00:00:08,158 --> 00:00:10,092
Every address has two numbers.

5
00:00:10,092 --> 00:00:11,648
A row, and a column.

6
00:00:12,098 --> 00:00:16,180
Diya is revising at night with
a periodic table on her desk.

7
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:18,873
Sodium sits in row three, column one.

8
00:00:19,223 --> 00:00:21,299
Potassium sits right below it.

9
00:00:21,299 --> 00:00:23,477
Both react violently with water.

10
00:00:23,977 --> 00:00:27,792
By the end of this video, you will
read any element's address straight

11
00:00:27,792 --> 00:00:28,994
from its electrons.

12
00:00:29,644 --> 00:00:31,965
So how do electrons give an address?

13
00:00:32,315 --> 00:00:37,485
Electrons fill shells, and shells
hold subshells, named s, p, d and f.

14
00:00:37,835 --> 00:00:42,490
The electronic configuration is the
list of where every electron sits.

15
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:45,141
Three things in it give the address.

16
00:00:45,141 --> 00:00:49,194
The outermost shell, the last
subshell, and the valence electrons.

17
00:00:49,194 --> 00:00:52,259
Valence electrons are the
ones in the outer shell.

18
00:00:52,859 --> 00:00:55,787
Let's build sodium on our molecule bench.

19
00:00:55,787 --> 00:00:57,860
Sodium has eleven electrons.

20
00:00:58,210 --> 00:01:00,151
Two go into the first shell.

21
00:01:00,151 --> 00:01:01,051
One s two.

22
00:01:01,449 --> 00:01:03,443
Eight go into the second shell.

23
00:01:03,443 --> 00:01:05,068
Two s two, two p six.

24
00:01:05,468 --> 00:01:08,378
The last one starts a
brand new third shell.

25
00:01:08,378 --> 00:01:09,380
Three s one.

26
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:12,143
The outermost shell is number three.

27
00:01:12,143 --> 00:01:14,260
So sodium sits in period three.

28
00:01:14,660 --> 00:01:17,578
The last electron went into an s subshell.

29
00:01:17,578 --> 00:01:19,712
So sodium is in the s block.

30
00:01:20,112 --> 00:01:22,253
And it has one valence electron.

31
00:01:22,253 --> 00:01:23,911
So it is in group one.

32
00:01:24,561 --> 00:01:27,142
Now chlorine, with seventeen electrons.

33
00:01:27,142 --> 00:01:28,919
Two, eight, and seven.

34
00:01:29,319 --> 00:01:31,781
Its outer shell is also number three.

35
00:01:31,781 --> 00:01:34,693
Period three again,
the same row as sodium.

36
00:01:35,093 --> 00:01:38,154
But its last electron
went into a p subshell.

37
00:01:38,154 --> 00:01:40,338
So chlorine is in the p block.

38
00:01:40,988 --> 00:01:45,144
Now compare sodium, two eight one,
and potassium, two eight eight one.

39
00:01:45,544 --> 00:01:46,716
Pause and think.

40
00:01:46,716 --> 00:01:50,740
Are sodium and potassium in the
same group, or the same period?

41
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:54,894
Same group!

42
00:01:54,894 --> 00:01:57,021
Each has one valence electron.

43
00:01:57,021 --> 00:02:02,095
But potassium has four shells, so it
sits one row lower, in period four.

44
00:02:02,745 --> 00:02:05,124
Here is the method, one step at a time.

45
00:02:05,474 --> 00:02:06,401
Step one.

46
00:02:06,401 --> 00:02:08,932
Write the electronic configuration.

47
00:02:09,682 --> 00:02:10,582
Step two.

48
00:02:10,489 --> 00:02:13,523
The biggest shell
number, n, is the period.

49
00:02:14,273 --> 00:02:15,180
Step three.

50
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:18,725
The subshell that takes the
last electron is the block.

51
00:02:19,475 --> 00:02:20,393
Step four.

52
00:02:20,393 --> 00:02:24,854
For the s block, the group is
the number of outer s electrons.

53
00:02:25,604 --> 00:02:29,545
For the p block, add ten to
the outer s and p electrons.

54
00:02:29,545 --> 00:02:31,451
Chlorine has two plus five.

55
00:02:31,451 --> 00:02:33,767
Ten plus seven is group seventeen.

56
00:02:34,517 --> 00:02:39,349
For the d block, add the outer s
electrons to the d electrons just below.

57
00:02:39,999 --> 00:02:42,787
This also explains the length of each row.

58
00:02:42,787 --> 00:02:44,955
Each orbital holds two electrons.

59
00:02:45,355 --> 00:02:47,726
Period two fills two s and two p.

60
00:02:47,726 --> 00:02:50,534
That is four orbitals, so eight elements.

61
00:02:50,934 --> 00:02:54,341
Period four fills four
s, three d and four p.

62
00:02:54,341 --> 00:02:56,949
Nine orbitals, so eighteen elements.

63
00:02:57,599 --> 00:02:58,648
Remember this.

64
00:02:58,648 --> 00:03:00,716
The outer shell gives the row.

65
00:03:00,716 --> 00:03:02,979
The last electron gives the block.

66
00:03:03,679 --> 00:03:05,415
Now, one worked example.

67
00:03:05,715 --> 00:03:09,479
Find the address of the element
with atomic number twenty six.

68
00:03:09,979 --> 00:03:11,166
Fill in order.

69
00:03:11,166 --> 00:03:16,658
One s two, two s two, two p six,
three s two, three p six, four s two,

70
00:03:16,658 --> 00:03:17,739
three d six.

71
00:03:18,489 --> 00:03:20,641
The biggest shell number is four.

72
00:03:20,641 --> 00:03:21,765
So period four.

73
00:03:22,515 --> 00:03:25,160
The last electrons went into three d.

74
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:26,461
So the d block.

75
00:03:27,211 --> 00:03:31,097
Group is two outer s electrons
plus six d electrons.

76
00:03:31,097 --> 00:03:32,189
Group eight.

77
00:03:32,939 --> 00:03:35,326
Period four, group eight, d block.

78
00:03:35,326 --> 00:03:37,227
That address belongs to iron.

79
00:03:37,877 --> 00:03:39,343
Now, the exam trap.

80
00:03:39,693 --> 00:03:42,027
Chlorine has seven valence electrons.

81
00:03:42,027 --> 00:03:44,633
So some students put it in group seven.

82
00:03:45,033 --> 00:03:47,624
But chlorine is a p block element.

83
00:03:47,624 --> 00:03:48,524
Add ten.

84
00:03:48,465 --> 00:03:49,744
Group seventeen.

85
00:03:50,394 --> 00:03:51,892
Where is this asked?

86
00:03:51,892 --> 00:03:53,753
JEE Main asks it directly.

87
00:03:53,753 --> 00:03:58,398
Give the configuration, or an atomic
number, and find the group, period

88
00:03:58,398 --> 00:03:59,298
or block.

89
00:03:59,822 --> 00:04:01,093
Exam level now.

90
00:04:01,093 --> 00:04:06,198
An element has the configuration
argon, three d ten, four s two, four p

91
00:04:06,198 --> 00:04:07,098
three.

92
00:04:06,855 --> 00:04:09,397
Find its period, block and group.

93
00:04:09,397 --> 00:04:10,722
Pause and solve.

94
00:04:16,122 --> 00:04:17,453
The answer is A.

95
00:04:17,453 --> 00:04:20,146
Period four, p block, group fifteen.

96
00:04:20,646 --> 00:04:22,798
The biggest shell number is four.

97
00:04:22,798 --> 00:04:23,922
So period four.

98
00:04:24,422 --> 00:04:26,896
The last electrons went into four p.

99
00:04:26,896 --> 00:04:28,139
So the p block.

100
00:04:28,139 --> 00:04:31,445
Three d is full, and does
not decide the block.

101
00:04:31,945 --> 00:04:35,063
Group is ten, plus two s, plus three p.

102
00:04:35,063 --> 00:04:35,963
Fifteen.

103
00:04:35,816 --> 00:04:37,736
D forgot to add the ten.

104
00:04:38,336 --> 00:04:39,793
Here is your recap.

105
00:04:39,793 --> 00:04:41,290
Screenshot this card.

106
00:04:44,490 --> 00:04:47,327
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

107
00:04:47,727 --> 00:04:48,627
One.

108
00:04:48,234 --> 00:04:51,397
Which part of the
configuration gives the period?

109
00:04:52,197 --> 00:04:53,097
Two.

110
00:04:52,744 --> 00:04:55,821
Why does period four
hold eighteen elements?

111
00:04:56,621 --> 00:04:57,521
Three.

112
00:04:57,264 --> 00:04:59,794
Chlorine has seven valence electrons.

113
00:04:59,794 --> 00:05:01,966
Why is it in group seventeen?

114
00:05:02,866 --> 00:05:07,315
Next time: why is a potassium
atom bigger than a sodium atom?

115
00:05:07,315 --> 00:05:10,156
We find out in atomic and ionic radii.
